Why does copying this C++ struct leave both objects pointing at the same text?

```cpp
#include <iostream>

struct Name {
    const char *p;
    Name(const char *s) : p(s) {}
};

int main() {
    Name a("Ana");
    Name b = a;                 // copies the pointer, not the text
    std::cout << (a.p == b.p) << "\n";
}
```

Don't manage raw memory at all. Use `std::string` or `std::vector`, which copy deeply and free themselves:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

struct Name {
    std::string s;
};

int main() {
    Name a{"Ana"};
    Name b = a;
    b.s += "!";
    std::cout << a.s << " " << b.s << "\n";
}
```

Output: `Ana Ana!`. Each object owns its own data, so the copy is independent and there is nothing to free by hand (the rule of zero).

